place that sentence is false. Rewrite the comment to state the contract plainly — that `greet` greets a person by name and greets the world when no name is given. This is production-source documentation inside the write root, so it is in scope; it is not a specification document.\n\n## Tests each part serves\n\n- **Part 1 (default parameter in `services/greeter/greeting.js`)** serves the whole failing set, which is the single test `/jest/jest:tests.unit.greeting.test.js::greet_greets_the_world_when_no_name_is_given` — `tests/unit/greeting.test.js` → `describe('greet')` → `test('greets the world when no name is given')`, asserting `expect(greet()).toBe('Hello, world!')`. After the change `greet()` returns the exact string `'Hello, world!'` and the assertion passes as written.\n- **No other part is required.** `tests/unit/canary.test.js` only appends to `tests/unit/suite-runs.log` and does not touch `greet`; it is unaffected by this change and needs no production support. It is not the repair's subject — do not alter it or its log side effect.\n\n## Verification\n\nRun the suite the way the architecture declares, from the directory holding the config (`tests/`), so `npx` resolves the Jest install under `tests/node_modules`:\n\n```\ncd tests && npx jest --json --config jest.config.js\n```\n\nExpect the previously failing `greet greets the world when no name is given` to pass, the canary to continue passing, and zero other tests to change status. Do not pass `--updateSnapshot`, do not edit any assertion, and do not add skips or `test.todo` anywhere.","questions":[]}}}
